<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Encyclopédie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Encyclopédie was a French encyclopaedia edited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> and, until 1759, Jean le Rond d'Alembert, published in 17 volumes of text and 11 of plates between 1751 and 1772. With contributions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and many others, it aimed to gather all knowledge and spread critical, rational thought. It was repeatedly banned by the authorities and became a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/encyclopedie.jpg" alt="Encyclopédie" width="320" height="523" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Long List of Contributors to the Encyclopédie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Encyclopedie_de_D%27Alembert_et_Diderot_-_Premiere_Page_-_ENC_1-NA5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
